    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d218">

                <pb facs="0413" n="351" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg351"/>

                <head>No. 213.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Sir Edward
                        Thornton</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1878-07-31"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 31,
                        1878</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Having submitted for the consideration of
                    the Secretary of the Treasury a copy of your note of the 11th instant relative
                    to the rules and regulations governing the transportation of dutiable
                    merchandise between the United States and Canada, I have now the honor to state
                    that I have received a communication from my colleague, dated the 27th instant,
                    stating that his Department concurs in your suggestion that a previous
                    announcement should be made by each government to the other of the precise
                    nature of any change in the regulations which may be <pb facs="0414" n="352"
                        xml:id="pg352"/> contemplated, and that the action of the Treasury
                    Department will be in accordance therewith.</p>
                <p>The Secretary of the Treasury suggests that the amendments to the regulations
                    referred to in the inclosed circulars, dated September 15, 1876, and November
                    27, 1876, may be submitted for the consideration of the Government of the
                    Dominion of Canada.</p>
                <p>I shall be happy to notify the Secretary of the Treasury of any action thereon
                    which may be taken by that government when it shall have been made known to
                    you.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
